course: biology
lesson: 41
title: Counting animals: eyes, ears, hearts, trunks, tentacles, teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Counting an animal's parts is a real scientist's game: how many hearts, eyes, legs or teeth it has tells you how it lives, and the numbers are often stranger than any cartoon.
explain: We have one heart, two eyes and two ears on the head, but an octopus has three hearts, a scallop can have about 200 eyes, and a cricket hears with ears on its knees.
explain: Wiggle your wobbly milk tooth in the bathroom mirror and count: you have 20 milk teeth and will get just one more set, while a shark keeps growing new teeth all its life like a conveyor belt.
explain: In cartoons octopuses wave «tentacles», every spider has two big eyes and the centipede proudly wears 100 shoes; real animals break every one of those rules, so always count for yourself.
fact: An octopus has 3 hearts: two small gill hearts push its blue, copper-based blood through the gills, and one main heart pumps it round the body; copper-based blood carries oxygen less well than our red blood, so the extra pumps help.
fact: An earthworm has 5 pairs of simple muscular tubes near its front end that squeeze its red blood along, often called its 5 pairs of hearts; it has no lungs and breathes through its damp skin.
fact: Most spiders have 8 eyes, usually in two or three rows; some kinds have 6 or fewer and some cave spiders have none, and many web-builders feel the world through the vibrations of their silk.
fact: A scallop can have up to about 200 tiny eyes, often bright blue dots, along the edge of its body between the two shells; each focuses light with a tiny curved mirror, and it spots shadows such as a hungry starfish, and then swims away by clapping its two shells together.
fact: An elephant has 1 trunk, its nose and upper lip joined into one boneless tube with tens of thousands of muscle bundles, often counted at about 40,000; it sucks up a few litres of water at a time and squirts it into its mouth.
fact: A squid has 8 arms and 2 long tentacles with suckers only on the club at their tips; an octopus has 8 arms and no tentacles at all.
fact: A shark has several rows of teeth that move forward like a conveyor belt, and it can lose and regrow thousands of teeth in its life, so fossil shark teeth are common on some beaches.
fact: A snail eats with a radula, a ribbon covered in thousands of tiny teeth, often quoted at about 14,000 for a garden snail, which rasps food like a file.
fact: Children have 20 milk teeth, which fall out roughly from age 6 to 12; adults have 32 teeth if all four wisdom teeth come in, and a lost adult tooth never grows back.
fact: Most starfish have 5 arms with a simple eye at the tip of each, some sun stars have 20 or more, and a starfish can slowly regrow a lost arm, which is why one arm may be shorter; look at rock-pool animals and leave them where they are.
fact: A cricket has 2 ears, tiny drum-like patches just below the knees of its front legs; grasshoppers have their ears on the sides of the belly.
fact: No centipede has exactly 100 legs: they have from 15 to 191 pairs, always an odd number of pairs; the leggiest known animal is an Australian millipede found in 2021 with 1,306 legs.
exercise: How many hearts does an octopus have? | 3 | 1 | 2 | 8
exercise: Why does an octopus need extra hearts? | Its copper-based blood carries oxygen less well, so extra pumps push it through the gills | Each of its eight arms needs its own heart to keep the blood moving to the tip | Its blood is red and very thick, so one heart alone could never push it round its soft body | It keeps two spare hearts in case the main one gets tired or breaks
exercise: What does an earthworm use instead of lungs? | Its damp skin | Two tiny lungs near its head | Gills along its sides | Its five pairs of hearts
exercise: How many eyes do most spiders have? | 8 | 2 | 6 | 4
exercise: How do many web-building spiders mostly know what is happening around them? | They feel vibrations in their silk with their legs | They see every detail sharply with their eight big eyes | They hear sounds with a pair of ears on their head | They smell the air around the web with their fangs
exercise: What are the rows of tiny blue dots along the edge of a scallop shell? | Eyes, up to about 200 of them | Breathing holes that let water in | Baby scallops waiting to hatch | Spots of paint that warn fish away
exercise: How does an elephant drink? | It sucks water into its trunk and squirts it into its mouth | It drinks through its trunk all the way, just like a long straw | It only licks the morning dew off wet leaves and grass | It never drinks and gets all of its water from food
exercise: What is an elephant's trunk? | Its nose and upper lip joined together, with no bones | A very long front tooth that went soft and bendy | An extra leg that slowly learned how to grab things | Its tongue, which grew so long it came out of its mouth
exercise: How can you tell a squid from an octopus by counting? | A squid has 8 arms and 2 long tentacles, an octopus 8 arms and no tentacles | A squid has 10 hearts and 2 brains, an octopus has 3 hearts and 1 brain | A squid has 6 arms and 2 fins, an octopus has 8 arms and no fins at all | They both have 8 tentacles and no arms, so you have to count their eyes instead
exercise: Why are fossil shark teeth so common on some beaches? | Sharks lose and regrow thousands of teeth in their lives | Sharks pull out their own teeth to mark where they live | Each shark has exactly 100 big teeth that fall out together | Shark teeth are made of stone from the very start
exercise: What does a garden snail use to scrape a lettuce leaf? | A radula, a ribbon with thousands of tiny teeth | Two big front teeth, just like a rabbit's | Its hard shell, rubbed back and forth against the leaf | Sharp little claws hidden under its body
exercise: How many milk teeth does a child have? | 20 | 32 | 28 | 24
exercise: Why does one arm of a starfish in a rock pool look shorter than the others? | It is probably growing back after being lost | Starfish are always born with one short arm | It is the special arm the starfish eats with | The starfish is slowly shrinking with old age
exercise: Where are a cricket's ears? | Just below the knees of its front legs | On top of its head, like a cat's ears | Inside the tips of its two long antennae | It has no ears at all and cannot hear
exercise: Why can you never count exactly 100 legs on a centipede? | Centipedes always have an odd number of pairs of legs | Centipedes always have exactly 1,000 legs, not 100 | Their legs move far too fast for anyone to count them | They drop a few legs as soon as you start to look
exercise: How many arms does a usual starfish have, and where are its eyes? | 5 arms, with a simple eye at each tip | 5 arms, with two eyes in the middle | 8 arms, with no eyes anywhere on it | 6 arms, with one round eye on each side
test: «octopus has 8 tentacles innit» What is right? | No, an octopus has 8 arms; squids have the 2 tentacles | Yes, every single limb of an octopus is a tentacle | No, an octopus has 10 tentacles, same as any squid has | Yes, and it has 2 more short arms on its back
test: In a cartoon a spider has two huge cute eyes. What would a real spider most likely have? | 8 eyes in two or three rows | 2 eyes, just like the cartoon | 1 big eye in the middle | 100 tiny eyes all over its body
test: «my bug book calls it a hundred-legger, so it's got 100 legs, end of» What is right? | No centipede has exactly 100 legs, the pairs are always odd | Yes, every centipede in the world has exactly 100 legs, mate | Yes, but only the grown-up ones, babies have fewer | No, all centipedes have exactly 1,000 legs instead
test: «lost my big tooth playing footie, it'll grow back like a shark's» What is right? | No, adult teeth never grow back; sharks regrow theirs, we do not | Yes, people grow a whole new set of teeth every ten years or so | Yes, but only if you brush the gap twice every single day | No, but a shark tooth can be glued into the gap instead
test: What would happen to an earthworm if its skin dried out in the sun? | It could not breathe and would die | It would breathe through its lungs instead | Its hearts would beat faster and save it | Nothing, worms love hot dry ground
test: «pulled a starfish off the rock to take home, it's fine, it'll grow a new arm anyway» What is the best answer? | Leave starfish on their rocks; look, count the arms, then let them be | Fine, starfish feel nothing at all and always grow back whole | Fine, as long as you keep it in a nice dry box with some sand | Only take the ones that already have a short arm, they're used to it
test: In a cartoon, an elephant drinks a whole lake through its trunk like a straw. What does a real elephant do? | Sucks a few litres into its trunk and squirts it into its mouth | Drinks through the trunk exactly like a straw, only slower | Lowers its head and drinks the water up with its tusks | Never drinks at all, it gets all its water from grass
test: «crap, cricket's chirping right by my head, where's its ears even» What is right? | On its front legs, just below the knees | On its head, tucked under its antennae | On its back, hidden under its wings | It hears through its mouth, no ears
test: A scallop has no head. Why might it still need eyes? | To see a starfish's shadow and swim away by clapping its shell | To read the numbers on the tags at the fishmonger's shop | To find its way back home to the head it once had | It does not need them, they are only there for decoration
test: Count them up: an octopus and an earthworm sit side by side in a cartoon. How many hearts together, if the worm's five pairs count as hearts? | 13 | 8 | 4 | 11
test: «shut up, a snail hasn't got any teeth» What is right? | It has thousands of tiny teeth on its radula | It has two big front teeth like a rabbit | It has exactly 20 teeth, the same as a child | It has no teeth and just sucks up juice
exam: State the number of hearts possessed by an octopus and identify the function of the smaller ones. | Three hearts, of which the two smaller propel the blood through the gills | One heart only, which serves the whole of the body and all eight arms | Two hearts, of which each one supplies blood to one of the two eyes | Eight hearts, of which each one serves a single arm of the animal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the limbs of the squid as distinct from those of the octopus? | The squid possesses eight arms and two tentacles; the octopus, eight arms and none | Both animals possess eight tentacles and no arms, being indistinguishable thereby | The squid possesses six arms and two fins, whereas the octopus possesses eight arms | The octopus possesses two tentacles and eight arms, whereas the squid possesses none
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the legs of the centipede. | The pairs of legs are invariably odd in number, so one hundred legs never occurs | Every centipede possesses precisely one hundred legs, as its common name states | Every centipede possesses precisely one thousand legs, ten for each segment | The number of legs increases by one pair with each year of the animal's age
exam: Ascertain the number of teeth comprising the first, or milk, dentition of a human child. | Twenty | Thirty-two | Twenty-eight | Twenty-four
